### Module 12 — Foundry-hosted Responses agent
# Hit this against either:
#   - your local dotnet run / docker run  (set @host = http://localhost:8088)
#   - the deployed Foundry agent endpoint (set @host = your project endpoint
#     AND @path = the agent-scoped subpath; see the "cloud" examples at the
#     bottom of this file)

@host = https://tripbot-foundry.services.ai.azure.com/api/projects/tripbot-project/agents/trip-planner/endpoint/protocols/openai
@path = /responses
@agent = trip-planner
@apiVersion = 2025-05-01

# When @host points at the deployed Foundry endpoint, every request must
# include `Authorization: Bearer {{token}}`. Mint a token (60-min lifetime):
#   az account get-access-token --resource https://ai.azure.com --query accessToken -o tsv
@token = eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsIng1dCI6IndoMDZzRWt6TEhKNXNOTmFVeVJZMl82TzhLMCIsImtpZCI6IndoMDZzRWt6TEhKNXNOTmFVeVJZMl82TzhLMCJ9..uSkSzp-zQjiz0WX9G5fqNMwhU1xZXqrqvGVUBYXoUcgIuYMpY6KGHa14yUq76xHXdUBN8SBd_R0nO-dk31Le34W9P18CasBkV41fOKrinl--NWKw8ls4Qw2CUS0jWp4P3FUCR7y7Avre0_AuLIH0ite-nxw9TCXMj6k_DAWgB11A4eNdC2P7sOH6GkxSc5FSBYN3ZWzjhFcg2FrkPHjlCzX1TEjOVQiqlyRq4fGlnknUQP2G2jLdtQWki__IHjQr2X-_clRxsJjOqvaaIopsxXjYXUl6S_UkAiIWMLFRugeL-kDQl_OKXaZPMv0YPPObcMuSmdv0vFnGsRuCYeTSvQ

# Foundry's hosting layer scopes sessions by these two headers. In production
# Foundry sets them per user/conversation; locally you pick any stable strings.
# Without them the server returns HTTP 500 (isolation key provider returns null).
# Change @chatKey when you want to start a fresh conversation.
@userKey = local-dev-user
@chatKey = local-dev-chat

### 1. Simple non-streaming request
POST {{host}}{{path}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
x-agent-user-isolation-key: {{userKey}}
x-agent-chat-isolation-key: {{chatKey}}

{
  "input": "Plan a 3-day trip to Tokyo in cherry blossom season.",
  "model": "{{agent}}"
}

### 2. Streaming request
# ⚠️ The VS Code REST Client does NOT render SSE incrementally. It buffers the
# entire stream in memory and only displays it after the server closes the
# connection — so for a long completion you'll stare at "running…" for 20-40s.
# That's a UI limitation, not a hung server.
#
# To watch tokens actually arrive, run this in a terminal instead:
#
#   curl --no-buffer -X POST http://localhost:8088/responses \
#     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#     -H "x-agent-user-isolation-key: local-dev-user" \
#     -H "x-agent-chat-isolation-key: local-dev-chat" \
#     -d '{"input":"Suggest a one-day food crawl in Lisbon.","model":"trip-planner","stream":true}'
POST {{host}}{{path}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
x-agent-user-isolation-key: {{userKey}}
x-agent-chat-isolation-key: {{chatKey}}

{
  "input": "Suggest a one-day food crawl in Lisbon.",
  "model": "{{agent}}",
  "stream": true
}

### 3. Continue the conversation
# Pass the previous response.id in `previous_response_id` to keep the
# server-side conversation state going (the Responses-protocol equivalent of
# Module 10's `contextId`).
POST {{host}}{{path}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
x-agent-user-isolation-key: {{userKey}}
x-agent-chat-isolation-key: {{chatKey}}

{
  "input": "What about vegetarian options on that crawl?",
  "model": "{{agent}}",
  "previous_response_id": "<paste id from a previous response here>"
}

###############################################################################
# Deployed-on-Foundry recipe
#
# When hitting the hosted agent, the URL shape is different from local. The
# request goes to your *project endpoint*, not the agent's own hostname, and
# the path is scoped under /agents/{name}/endpoint/protocols/openai/...
# A `Foundry-Features: HostedAgents=V1Preview` header is REQUIRED during the
# preview — without it you get HTTP 400 `preview_feature_required`.
#
# To switch the requests above to cloud mode, override the variables:
#   1) @host  → your project endpoint (NOT the agent hostname). Example:
#               https://tripbot-foundry.services.ai.azure.com/api/projects/tripbot-project
#   2) @path  → /agents/{{agent}}/endpoint/protocols/openai/responses?api-version={{apiVersion}}
#   3) @token → az account get-access-token --resource https://ai.azure.com --query accessToken -o tsv
#
# Then also add `Foundry-Features: HostedAgents=V1Preview` to each request
# header block (or use the standalone example below).
###############################################################################

### 4. Deployed agent — non-streaming
# Set @host and @path to the cloud values described above before sending.
POST {{host}}/agents/{{agent}}/endpoint/protocols/openai/responses?api-version={{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Foundry-Features: HostedAgents=V1Preview

{
  "input": "Plan a 3-day trip to Tokyo in cherry blossom season.",
  "model": "{{agent}}"
}
